    "summary": "Rare acoustic set with So Sad cover, strong electric jams, New Riders cameo",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a rare acoustic segment featuring the Everly Brothers cover “So Sad (To Watch Good Love Go Bad)” and gospel‑tinged numbers. The electric set erupts with powerful guitar work on “New Speedway Boogie” and carries through high‑energy jams into a spirited New Riders of the Purple Sage appearance. Vocal harmonies shine on “Good Lovin’,” and Pigpen adds keyboard textures despite his laryngitis.",
    "review": "July 11, 1970 at the Fillmore East captured one of the Dead’s most eclectic evenings. After an intimate acoustic set that included the seldom‑heard Everly Brothers cover “So Sad (To Watch Good Love Go Bad)” and gospel‑flavored numbers like “Swing Low,” the band launched into a thunderous electric second set. Jerry Garcia’s heavy guitar, peppered with pedal‑steel licks and an early wah‑wah effect, drove the room from the opening “New Speedway Boogie” through a sprawling jam that built momentum for the high‑octane New Riders of the Purple Sage cameo. Bob Weir took lead on “Good Lovin’,” adding tight harmonies on the memorable line “out where the horses were tied,” while Pigpen, battling laryngitis, still managed to weave keyboard textures into the mix. The night ends with classic Dead staples—“Morning Dew,” “Uncle John’s Band” and a seamless “China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider”—making this show a must‑listen despite its modest audience‑tape fidelity.",
